  "answer": "<p>The <b>first option</b> is correct because Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose now supports data transformation via Lambda functions with an invocation timeout of up to 30 minutes, significantly higher than the previous 5-minute limit. This increase enables more complex log processing and transformation tasks before delivery to storage and analytics services like Amazon Athena and QuickSight. By allowing the DeliveryStreamType property (without extra configuration details such as KinesisStreamAsSource), you streamline ingestion from the given source. Using Athena and QuickSight, data scientists can efficiently run queries and build data visualizations on the log data delivered.<br><br><i>The second option</i> is incorrect because it incorrectly combines KinesisStreamAsSource with a CloudWatch Logs SubscriptionFilter as a delivery stream destination; Firehose expects a direct connection based on the source type, and this hybrid setup is not standard. <i>The third option</i> mentions using AWS Glue crawlers and databases for discovering Lambda transformations, which is misleading—AWS Glue isn't responsible for discovering Kinesis Firehose Lambda transforms; it's used for data cataloging, not Lambda management. It also references the outdated 5-minute Lambda timeout. <i>The fourth option</i> incorrectly states that the Lambda function can only run for up to 5 minutes, which ignores the updated 30-minute timeout, and sets DeliveryStreamType as KinesisStreamAsSource, which may not be suitable for direct log stream ingestion.<br><br><b>Tip:</b> Always check official AWS documentation for service limits and new features, as capabilities—like Lambda invocation limits within Kinesis Firehose—can change. Knowing the correct settings for DeliveryStreamType and recognizing which service is responsible for which aspect of the pipeline ensures a robust and maintainable architecture.</p>",
  "query": "<p>A serverless mobile app designed using the AWS Well-Architected Framework is under review to discover ways to enhance user experience. The analytics and machine learning team, comprising data scientists, needs to develop dashboards, run SQL-style queries, and generate visualizations from log data to guide business decisions.<br>You are the DevOps engineer engaged in supporting the architecture. What key considerations are needed to design the log delivery pipeline for fulfilling these requirements?</p>",
  "original_oid": "6793da1561969b416038a145",
